    "result_text": "All 282 tests pass with **BUILD SUCCESS** (the single `ERROR` log line is an expected message from a negative-path test, not a failure).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` following the exact pattern the codebase already uses for creation-time derived fields (`membershipNumber`, `customerCode`, `membershipTier`):\n\n1. **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new function) \u2014 counts owners already sharing the new owner's last name. It calls `findByLastName` (a prefix match) and filters for exact last-name equality, then sets the count. Since the new owner isn't saved yet, every match is a distinct pre-existing namesake \u2014 giving \"other owners\" at that moment.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the transactional pipeline between `assignMembershipTier` and `save`.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column field with getter/setter, so MapStruct auto-maps it into `OwnerDto` (same as the other creation fields, which is why no mapper change was needed).\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` schema property (`integer`, `int32`, `minimum: 0`, `readOnly`), which regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field.\n\n5. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column so the value persists and is returned identically from both the POST response and later GETs.\n\nThe pre-existing checkpoint test `Cp16Tests` (0 namesakes for the first owner of a surname, 1 for the second) passes, as does the full suite.",
